messages:
maria_cs: Heads-up: onboarding run for NimbusPay is failing during frontend warmup. They're seeing sustained 429s at SDK initialization.

maria_cs: Logs show many parallel preflight calls from the browser cluster. Example: 
```
2026-03-20T09:03:10Z ERROR preflight id=rx1 status=429 msg="quota exceeded" retry_after=25
2026-03-20T09:03:11Z ERROR preflight id=rx2 status=429 msg="quota exceeded" retry_after=25
```

maria_cs: Their engineering lead says the SPA spins up ~90 workers each doing preflight on load. Any rapid mitigations we can propose?

stella_support: Quick list: 1) add client-side splay + jitter; 2) limit concurrent preflights (6-12); 3) implement short-lived token caching or a tiny edge proxy to reduce token fanout; 4) we can temporarily increase their burst quota while they warm up.

kai_integration: Confirmed they're fetching ephemeral tokens per worker. TTL is currently 12s in their config. That explains the refresh storms.

jon_devex: For immediate relief: ask them to patch the client to reuse a single cached token for N seconds and cap parallel preflight. Example patch suggestion: 
```
// simple browser cache (pseudo)
let cachedToken = null;
let tokenExpiry = 0;
async function getToken() {
  if (cachedToken && Date.now() < tokenExpiry - 5000) return cachedToken;
  const t = await fetch('/get-ephemeral');
  cachedToken = t.token; tokenExpiry = Date.now() + t.ttl*1000;
  return cachedToken;
}
// ensure only M workers refresh in parallel
const refreshSemaphore = new Semaphore(3);
```

stella_support: Also include client jitter on init: randomized delay scaling with worker index. Sharing snippet: 
```
// stagger init
for (let i=0;i<workers.length;i++) {
  const base = 200 * (i % 10);
  const jitter = Math.random()*800;
  setTimeout(() => workers[i].preflight(), base + jitter);
}
```

maria_cs: Can we recommend a temporary token TTL increase? Their security folks might push back but it's likely the fastest fix.

jon_devex: Recommend two safe options: 1) temporarily set ephemeral_token_ttl to 45-60s for onboarding (monitor); or 2) add a tiny server-side proxy that caches tokens for 30s so clients don't request directly. Document both so they can choose.

kai_integration: I'll apply a one-hour +40% burst quota for account_nimbus in case they need breathing room (will log change).

deploy-bot: Quota change applied: account_nimbus burst +40% for 1 hour (ticket=nimbus-20260320-burst) :white_check_mark:

(5m later)

kai_integration: Devs patched client to cache tokens and cap preflight to 8 concurrent. Early telemetry: preflight 429 rate dropped from ~22% to 0.6%. Logs now show mostly 200s.

maria_cs: Awesome. Any remaining flakiness?

kai_integration: A few stragglers during the first 30s of cold starts but within acceptable thresholds. We'll keep quota bump for 30 more mins and then roll back.

stella_support: I'll draft a short enablement blurb: "Why preflight storms happen, short-term mitigations (splay, token cache, quota bump), and production best practices (proxy, TTL policy, connection reuse)."

jon_devex: I'll update the browser SDK docs with a new "Onboarding warmup" section showing the token-cache snippet and an example https.Agent keepAlive for Node-side SSR.

maria_cs: Scheduling a 15-min sync with Nimbus engineers tomorrow to walk through fixes and collect feedback for docs. Thanks all.

kai_integration: Confirming stable at 22m post-patch. Closing from infra side. :thumbsup:
